Gamma oscillations in the human EEG are typically defined as occurring in the frequency range of **30-100 Hz**, with the most common range being **30-60 Hz**.

The exact frequency range can vary depending on the specific definition and the brain signal being studied. Some neuro scientists may use a slightly broader range, such as 30-120 Hz.

The gamma band is further subdivided into low gamma (30-60 Hz) and high gamma (60-100 Hz or even higher). These subbands may be processed differently by the brain and may be involved in different cognitive processes.

Thus, the frequency range of gamma oscillations in the human EEG is **30-100 Hz**, with the most commonly considered range being **30-60 Hz**.
