","The entropy change (ΔS) for a reaction is calculated using the following formula:

ΔS = ΣS_products - ΣS_reactants

where S_products and S_reactants are the molar entropies of the products and reactants, respectively.

Given the reaction:
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]

With the molar entropies:
\[ \text{PbS(s)} = 91.2 \, \text{J/K mol} \]
\[ \text{O}_2(g) = 205.1 \, \text{J/K mol} \]
\[ \text{PbO(s)} = 66.5 \, \text{J/K mol} \]
\[ \text{SO}_2(g) = 248.2 \, \text{J(K mol} \]

ΔS = (2 * S_PbO + 2 * S_SO2) - (2 * S_PbS + 3 * S_O2)

ΔS = (2 * 66.5 + 2 * 248.2) - (2 * 91.2 + 3 * 205.1)

ΔS = (133 + 496.4) - (182.4 + 615.3)

ΔS = 629.4 - 797.7

ΔS = -168.3 J/K mol

