","To find the probability of getting at head in at case 9 or more of the 12 flips, we can use the binomial probability formula:

\[
P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1 - p)^{n - k}.
\]
Here, $n = 12$ (number of flips), $p = 0.5$ (probability of getting a head), and $k$ is the number of heads.

We want to find the probability of getting at head in at case 9 or more of the 12 flips, which means $k = 9, 10, 11, or 12$. So, we need to find $P(X \geq 9) = P(X = 9) + P(X = 10) + P(X = 11) + P(X = 12)$.

\[
P(X = 9) = \binom{12}{9} (0.5)^9 (0.5)^{12 - 9} = \binom{12}{9} (0.5)^{9} (0.5)^{3} = \binom{12}{9} (0.5)^{12} = 220 \times (0.5)^{12} = \frac{220}{4096}
\]

\[
P(X = 10) = \binom{12}{10} (0.5)^{10} (0.5)^{12 - 10} = \binom{12}{10} (0.5)^{10} (0.5)^{2} = \binom{12}{10} (0.5)^{12} = 66 \times (0.5)^{12} = \frac{66}{4096}
\]

\[
P(X = 11) = \binom{12}{11} (0.5)^{11} (0.5)^{12 - 11} = \binom{12}{11} (0.5)^{11} (0.5)^{1} = \binom{12}{11} (0.5)^{12} = 12 \times (0.5)^{12} = \frac{12}{4096}
\]

\[
P(X = 12) = \binom{12}{12} (0.5)^{12} (0.5)^{12 - 12} = \binom{12}{12} (0.5)^{12} (0.5)^{0} = 1 \times (0.5)^{12} = 1 \times (0.5)^{12} = \frac{1}{4096}
\]

Now, we sum these probabilities:
\[
P(X \geq 9) = \frac{220}{4096} + \frac{66}{4096} + \frac{12}{4096} + \frac{1}{4096} = \frac{220 + 66 + 12 + 1}{4096} = \frac{299}{4096}
\]

Thus, the probability of getting at head in at case 9 or more of the 12 flips is:
\[
