Convert the relative speed to meters per second:
\[ 36 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 36 \times \frac{5}{18} \text{ m/s} = 10 \text{ m/s} \]
The jogher is initially 150 meters ahead of the train's edge. The time taken for the train to pass the jugher is 25 seconds. During this time, the train must cover the initial distance between the jogher and the back of the train (150 meters) and the length of the train (L meters). The total distance covered by the train relative to the jugher is:
\[ \text{Total distance} = 150 + L \text{ meters} \]
Using the formula:
\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} \]
\[ 150 + L = 10 \times 25 \]
\[ 150 + L = 250 \]
\[ L = 250 - 150 \]
\[ L = 100 \text{ meters} \]
Thus, the length of the train is 100 meters.

