","To find the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = P(x, y)\mathbf{i} + Q(x, y)\mathbf{j} + R(x, y)\mathbf{k}\), we use the definition of the curl:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial R}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial R}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Given that \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the \(z\)-component of \(\mathbf{V}\) is \(R(x, y) = 0\). Thus, the partial derivatives with respect to \(z\) are all zero:
\[
\frac{\partial P}{\partial z} = 0, \quad \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} = 0, \quad \frac{\partial R}{\partial z} = 0.
\]
Substituting these into the curl formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial 0} \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial 0} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Since \(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial 0} = 0\) and \(\frac{\partial P}{\partial 0} = 0\), the terms involving these derivatives are zero. Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Simplifying further:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = 0 \mathbf{i} - 0 \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
The curl is a vector field in the \(z\)-direction, and its component is the divergence of \(P\) and \(Q\) with respect to \(x\).

