What is the net chemical reaction that occurs during the discharging of a lead storage battery?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The net reaction during the discharging of a lead storage battery is:  
\[ \text{Pb(s) + PbO}_2\text{(s) + 4H}^+\text{(aq) + 2SO}_4^{2-}\text{(aq) → 2PbSO}_4\text{(s) + 2H}_2\text{O(l)}. \]  
In this reaction, sulfuric acid (\(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\)) is consumed, and lead sulfate (\(\text{PbSO}_4\)) and water are formed. This process converts chemical energy into electrical energy.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a cell in a liquid cell lead-acid cell is:  
\[ \text{\[\text{Pb(s) + 2H_2\text{SO}_4 (aq) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb\text{SO}_4 (s) + 2H^+(aq) + 2e^-\]}\]  
\[ \text{\[\text{Pb\text{O}_2 (s) + H_2\text{O} (l) + \text{HSO}_4^- (aq) \rightarrow \text{Pb\text{SO}_4 (s) + 2H^+ (aq)\]}\]  
**Combining these two half-reactions gives the overall reaction:**  
\[ \text{\[\text{Pb(s) + \text{Pb\text{O}_2 (s) + 2H_2\text{SO}_4 (aq) + 2H_2\text{O} (l) \rightarrow 2\text{Pb\text{SO}_4 (s) + 2H^+(aq) + 2H_2O(l)\]}\]  

**Simplified reaction (often used):**  
\[ \text{Pb + PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  
**Explanation:**  
- **At the anode (negative terminal):** Iron (Pb) is oxidized to lead sulfate (PbSO₄).  
- **At the cathode (positive terminal):** Lead dioxide (PbO₂) is reduced to lead sulfurate (PbSO₄).  
- The hydrogen ions (H⁺) and sulfate ions (HSO₄⁻) from the electrode reactions combine with water to maintain electrical neutrality in the cell.  
- As the device discharges, the hydrogen sulfate (HSO₄⁻) is converted to sulfate (SO₄²⁻).  

Thus, the correct answer is \(\boxed{\text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}\)).
