","1. **Calculate the Standard Free Energy Change for ATP Hydrolayically**
   The hydrolysis of ATP is given by the reaction:
   \[ \text{ATP} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{ADP} + \text{Pi} \]
   The standard free energy change for this reaction is given by:
   \[ \Delta G_{\text{ATP}} = -RT \ln Q \]
   where:
   - \( R = 8.314 \, \text{J/mol·K} \) (gas constant)
   - \( T = 37^\circ \text{C} = 310 \, \text{K} \) (temperature in Kelvin)
   - \( Q = \frac{[\text{ADP}][\text{Pi}]}{[\text{ATP}]} \) (reaction quotient)

   Since the concentrations of ATP, ADP, and Pi are all 1 mM, the reaction quotient \( Q \) is:
   \[ Q = \frac{(1 \times 10^{-3})(1 \times 10^{-3})}{1 \times 10^{-3}} = 1 \times 10^{-3} \]

   Substituting the values:
   \[ \Delta G_{\text{ATP}} = -8.314 \times 310 \times \ln(1 \times 10^{-3}) \]
   \[ \Delta G_{\text{ATP}} = -8.314 \times 310 \times (-6.908) \]
   \[ \Delta G_{\text{ATP}} = 17,600 \, \text{J/mol} \]
   \[ \Delta G_{\text{ATP}} = 17.6 \, \text{kJ/mol} \]

2. **Conclusion**
   The standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 310 K (37°C) under the given conditions (1 mM ATP, ADP, and Pi) is \(\boxed{17.6 \, \text{kJ/mol}}\).

**Significance in Energy Transfer in Living Systems**
